# Opens the logind session: authentication and account checks ran in
# ghostauth, on the ghostd stack (/etc/pam.d/ghostd,
# packaging/system/pam.d/ghostd.in), before ghostseat is ever started
# (docs/design/login-and-sessions.md). Installed as /etc/pam.d/ghostseat
# by `cmake --install` (GHOST_PAM_DIR).
# A template: pam-render.sh fills in the distro's stack names.
#
# The auth stack decides nothing -- pam_permit is its only required line.
# It exists for two reasons:
#
# - Context::open_session always calls pam_setcred(ESTABLISH_CRED) first
#   and aborts if it fails, and Linux-PAM resolves setcred through the
#   service's own auth stack. With no auth lines it would fall back to
#   /etc/pam.d/other's auth stack, which on distros where "other" is all
#   pam_deny would fail every open.
#
# - Unlocking the user's wallet/keyring at login, as a display manager's
#   stack does. ghostseat runs pam_authenticate once, answering the
#   password prompt with the password ghostd passes it in Open, but only when the
#   login is the one that opens the session. pam_unix is there only to
#   read that answer into PAM_AUTHTOK, the item the keyring modules take
#   the password from (applications can't set it themselves); if it fails
#   (an account pam_unix doesn't know), the wallet stays locked and the
#   login goes on. The keyring modules' auth half remembers the password,
#   their session half unlocks with it, which only works because both run
#   in this one PAM handle. The leading `-` skips a module that isn't
#   installed.
auth     optional   pam_unix.so nodelay
auth     required   pam_permit.so
-auth    optional   pam_gnome_keyring.so
-auth    optional   pam_kwallet5.so
session  include    @PAM_SESSION@
-session optional   pam_gnome_keyring.so auto_start
-session optional   pam_kwallet5.so auto_start
